Over one time period, \[s(t)=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{2A}{T}t, &amp; 0\le t\le \dfrac{T}{2},\\ -A, &amp; \dfrac{T}{2}&lt;t\le T.
\end{cases}\] The RMS value is \[S_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\int_{0}^{T}s^2(t)\,dt}.\] Substituting the waveform, \[S_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\left[
\int_{0}^{T/2}\left(\frac{2At}{T}\right)^2dt
+\int_{T/2}^{T}A^2\,dt
\right]}.\] Evaluating the integrals, \[S_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\left[
\frac{A^2T}{6}
+\frac{A^2T}{2}
\right]}
=\sqrt{\frac{2A^2}{3}}
=A\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}\,A}\]

The open-loop transfer function is \[G(s)H(s)
=
\frac{10K}{s(1+0.1s)(1+0.5s)}\] Nyquist contour poles are at: \[s=0,\qquad s=-2,\qquad s=-10\] Hence, the Nyquist contour contains an indentation around the pole at the origin. <div class="table-scroll"><table class="qtable"><tr><td>Segment</td><td>Mapping of \(G(j\omega)H(j\omega)\)</td></tr><tr><td>Segment--I</td><td>\(G(j0^+)H(j0^+) \rightarrow G(j\infty^+)H(j\infty^+)\)</td></tr><tr><td>Segment--II</td><td>\(G(j\infty^+)H(j\infty^+) \rightarrow G(j\infty^-)H(j\infty^-)\)</td></tr><tr><td>Segment--III</td><td>\(G(j\infty^-)H(j\infty^-) \rightarrow G(j0^-)H(j0^-)\)</td></tr><tr><td>Segment--IV</td><td>\(G(j0^-)H(j0^-) \rightarrow G(j0^+)H(j0^+)\)</td></tr></table></div> img/ch7_nyquist_plot/7_95_1_1.png <strong>Segment--I:</strong> Put \(s=j\omega\) in the given transfer function, \[G(j\omega)H(j\omega)
=
\frac{10K}{j\omega(1+j0.1\omega)(1+j0.5\omega)}
=
\frac{10K}
{\omega\sqrt{1+0.01\omega^2}\sqrt{1+0.25\omega^2}}
\angle
\left(
-90^\circ
-\tan^{-1}(0.1\omega)
-\tan^{-1}(0.5\omega)
\right)\] \[G(j0^+)H(j0^+)
=
\infty\angle(-90^\circ)\] \[G(j\infty^+)H(j\infty^+)
=
0\angle(-270^\circ)\] Again, \[G(j\omega)H(j\omega)
=
\frac{10K}
{j\omega(1+j0.6\omega-0.05\omega^2)}
=
\frac{10K}
{-0.6\omega^2+j\omega(1-0.05\omega^2)}\] Multiplying numerator and denominator by the conjugate, \[G(j\omega)H(j\omega)
=
\frac{10K\left[-0.6\omega^2-j\omega(1-0.05\omega^2)\right]}
{0.36\omega^4+\omega^2(1-0.05\omega^2)^2}
=
\frac{-6K}
{0.36\omega^2+(1-0.05\omega^2)^2}
-j\frac{10K(1-0.05\omega^2)}
{\omega\left[0.36\omega^2+(1-0.05\omega^2)^2\right]}\] Real part is negative for all \(\omega&gt;0\). Imaginary part changes sign when \[1-0.05\omega^2=0\] \[\omega=\sqrt{20}\] Therefore, the Nyquist plot crosses the real axis at \(\omega=\sqrt{20}\) Substituting \(\omega=\sqrt{20}\) in OLTF, \[G(j\sqrt{20})H(j\sqrt{20})
=
-\frac{5K}{6}\] Therefore, the Nyquist plot cuts the real axis at \(\left(-\dfrac{5K}{6},\,0\right)\) For \(0&lt;\omega&lt;\sqrt{20}\) \[1-0.05\omega^2&gt;0\] Hence imaginary part is negative. Therefore, the Nyquist plot lies in Quadrant--III. For \(\omega&gt;\sqrt{20}\) \[1-0.05\omega^2&lt;0\] Hence imaginary part is positive. Therefore, the Nyquist plot lies in Quadrant--II. <strong>Segment--II:</strong> Put \[s=Re^{j\theta},
\qquad
R\rightarrow\infty,
\qquad
\theta:90^\circ\rightarrow-90^\circ ~~\text{(CW)}\] \[G(Re^{j\theta})H(Re^{j\theta})
=
\lim_{R\rightarrow\infty}
\frac{10K}
{Re^{j\theta}(1+0.1Re^{j\theta})(1+0.5Re^{j\theta})}\] \[\approx
\lim_{R\rightarrow\infty}
\frac{200K}{R^3e^{j3\theta}}
=
0e^{-j3\theta}\] Hence the mapping is from \(-270^\circ\rightarrow270^\circ\) with approximately zero radius in anticlockwise direction. <strong>Segment--III:</strong> Segment--III is the mirror image of Segment--I. <strong>Segment--IV:</strong> Put \[s=re^{j\theta},
\qquad
r\rightarrow0,
\qquad
\theta:-90^\circ\rightarrow90^\circ~~\text{(ACW)}\] \[G(re^{j\theta})H(re^{j\theta})
=
\lim_{r\rightarrow0}
\frac{10K}
{re^{j\theta}(1+0.1re^{j\theta})(1+0.5re^{j\theta})}\] \[\approx
\lim_{r\rightarrow0}
\frac{10K}{re^{j\theta}}
=
\infty e^{-j\theta}\] Hence the mapping is from \(90^\circ\rightarrow-90^\circ\) with infinite radius in clockwise direction. Therefore, the complete Nyquist plot is <img src="img/ab8150cffc786570.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,384px)">

From Q7.95.1, the Nyquist plot crosses the negative real axis when \(\omega_{pc}=\sqrt{20}\) rad/s. At \(\omega=\sqrt{20}\,\text{rad/s}\), \[G(j\sqrt{20})H(j\sqrt{20})
=
-\frac{5K}{6}\] Hence, the phase crossover point is \(\left(-\dfrac{5K}{6},\,j0\right)\) Gain margin is defined as \[GM
=
\frac{1}
{\left|G(j\omega_{pc})H(j\omega_{pc})\right|}
=
\frac{1}{5K/6}
=
\frac{6}{5K}\] Therefore, \[\boxed{\omega_{pc}=\sqrt{20}\;\text{rad/s}} \qquad \text{and} \qquad \boxed{GM=\frac{6}{5K}}\] <img src="img/854f1eb9c46775e6.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,640px)">

Given, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
-4 &amp; -1\\
3 &amp; -1
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
B=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
1
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
C=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
D=0\] The transfer function is \[G(s)=C(sI-A)^{-1}B+D\] \[sI-A=
\begin{bmatrix}
s+4 &amp; 1\\
-3 &amp; s+1
\end{bmatrix}\] \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{(s+4)(s+1)+3}
\begin{bmatrix}
s+1 &amp; -1\\
3 &amp; s+4
\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{1}{s^2+5s+7}
\begin{bmatrix}
s+1 &amp; -1\\
3 &amp; s+4
\end{bmatrix}\] \[G(s)
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\frac{1}{s^2+5s+7}
\begin{bmatrix}
s+1 &amp; -1\\
3 &amp; s+4
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{1}{s^2+5s+7}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
s\\
s+7
\end{bmatrix}
=\frac{s}{s^2+5s+7}\] \[\boxed{(A)\;\dfrac{s}{s^2+5s+7}}\]

questions.question:
Answer Questions Q9.95.2 and Q9.95.3 using the following data. These two questions together carry a total of <strong>5 marks</strong>. The state equation of a linear time-invariant system is given by \[\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(t)\\ x_2(t)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 1\\
-1 &amp; -2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(t)\\ x_2(t)
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
r(t)\]<br/><br/>Find state transition \(\phi(t)\) and the characteristic equation of the system.

questions.explanation:
Given, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 1\\
-1 &amp; -2
\end{bmatrix}\] The characteristic equation is \[|sI-A|=0\] \[\left|
\begin{matrix}
s &amp; -1\\
1 &amp; s+2
\end{matrix}
\right|=0\] \[s(s+2)+1=0\] \[(s+1)^2=0\] Hence, the characteristic equation is \(s^2+2s+1=0\) The state transition matrix is \[\phi(t)=\mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{(sI-A)^{-1}\right\}
=
\mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{\frac{1}{(s+1)^2}
\begin{bmatrix}
s+2 &amp; 1\\
-1 &amp; s
\end{bmatrix}\right\}
=
\mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{\begin{bmatrix}
\dfrac{1}{s+1}+\dfrac{1}{(s+1)^2} &amp;
\dfrac{1}{(s+1)^2}\\ -\dfrac{1}{(s+1)^2} &amp;
\dfrac{1}{s+1}-\dfrac{1}{(s+1)^2}
\end{bmatrix}\right\}\] Therefore, \[\phi(t)
=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^{-t}+te^{-t} &amp; te^{-t}\\
-te^{-t} &amp; e^{-t}-te^{-t}
\end{bmatrix}\] Hence, \[\boxed{\text{Characteristic Equation: }s^2+2s+1=0}\] \[\boxed{
\phi(t)=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^{-t}+te^{-t} &amp; te^{-t}\\
-te^{-t} &amp; e^{-t}-te^{-t}
\end{bmatrix}
}\]

