The given problem describes an electromagnetic circuit with an E-shaped core and an armature. The total reluctance of the magnetic circuit depends entirely on the air gaps because the core material has infinite permeability, \(\mu_r=\infty\). Area of the central limb air gap is \[A_c=25\,\text{mm}\times 25\,\text{mm}
=625\times 10^{-6}\,\text{m}^2\] Length of central air gap is \[g_c=0.5\,\text{mm}=0.5\times 10^{-3}\,\text{m}\] Reluctance of central air gap is \[\mathcal{R}_{g,c}
=\frac{g_c}{\mu_0 A_c}
=\frac{0.5\times 10^{-3}}{(4\pi\times 10^{-7})(625\times 10^{-6})}
=\frac{2\times 10^6}{\pi}\,\text{AT/Wb}\] Each side limb has \[A_s=12.5\,\text{mm}\times 25\,\text{mm}
=312.5\times 10^{-6}\,\text{m}^2
=\frac{A_c}{2}\] Hence, \[\mathcal{R}_{g,s}
=\frac{g_c}{\mu_0 A_s}
=2\mathcal{R}_{g,c}\] The two side gaps are in parallel, therefore \[\mathcal{R}_{g,\text{side,eq}}
=\frac{\mathcal{R}_{g,s}}{2}
=\mathcal{R}_{g,c}\] Total equivalent reluctance is \[\mathcal{R}_{eq}
=\mathcal{R}_{g,c}+\mathcal{R}_{g,\text{side,eq}}
=2\mathcal{R}_{g,c}
=\frac{4\times 10^6}{\pi}\,\text{AT/Wb}\] Since winding resistance and core losses are neglected, \[V=2\pi fN\phi_{\text{rms}}\] \[\phi_{\text{rms}}
=\frac{V}{2\pi fN}
=\frac{230}{2\pi\times 50\times 1500}
=4.8808\times 10^{-4}\,\text{Wb}\] The current drawn is \[I=\frac{\phi_{\text{rms}}\mathcal{R}_{eq}}{N}\] \[I=\frac{(4.8808\times 10^{-4})\left(\dfrac{4\times 10^6}{\pi}\right)}{1500}
=0.414\,\text{A}\] For the central air gap, \[F_c=\frac{\phi^2}{2\mu_0 A_c}\] For each side gap, flux is \(\dfrac{\phi}{2}\) and area is \(\dfrac{A_c}{2}\), so \[F_s=\frac{\left(\dfrac{\phi}{2}\right)^2}{2\mu_0\left(\dfrac{A_c}{2}\right)}
=\frac{\phi^2}{4\mu_0 A_c}
=\frac{F_c}{2}\] Total instantaneous force is \[F=F_c+2F_s
=\frac{\phi^2}{\mu_0 A_c}\] Using RMS flux for average force, \[F_{\text{avg}}
=\frac{\phi_{\text{rms}}^2}{\mu_0 A_c}\] \[F_{\text{avg}}
=\frac{(4.8808\times 10^{-4})^2}{(4\pi\times 10^{-7})(625\times 10^{-6})}
=303.3\,\text{N}\] \[\boxed{0.414}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Since \(\mu_r=\infty\), reluctance of the core is neglected.</li><li>The central air gap is in series with the parallel combination of the two side air gaps.</li><li>For sinusoidal flux, \(V=2\pi fN\phi_{\text{rms}}\).</li><li>Average force is obtained using RMS flux.</li></ul></div>

For autotransformer starter with tapping \(K\): \[I_{\text{AT}}=K^{2}I_{\text{DOL}}\] For \(50\%\) tapping, \[I_{\text{AT}}=(0.5)^2I_{\text{DOL}}=0.25I_{\text{DOL}}\] For star-delta starter: \[I_{\text{SD}}=\frac{1}{3}I_{\text{DOL}}\] Hence, \[\frac{I_{\text{AT}}}{I_{\text{SD}}}
=
\frac{0.25}{1/3}
=
\frac{3}{4}\] \[\boxed{0.75}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Autotransformer starter line current is \(K^{2}\) times DOL current.</li><li>Star-delta starter line current is \(\dfrac{1}{3}\) times DOL current.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use \(K\) instead of \(K^{2}\) for supply current in autotransformer starting.</li><li>Convert \(50\%\) tapping into \(K=0.5\).</li></ul></div>

Under no-load condition: \[P_{\text{input}}=P_{c}=80\,\text{W}\] Core loss remains the same irrespective of the side on which it is measured. Mutual inductance referred to HV side: \[M_{\text{HV}}
=
1.91\times\left(\frac{400}{200}\right)\] \[M_{\text{HV}}=3.82\,\text{H}\] Magnetizing reactance on HV side: \[X_{m}
=
2\pi fM\] \[X_{m}
=
2\pi\times50\times3.82\] \[X_{m}=1200.088\,\Omega\] Magnetizing current: \[I_{\mu}
=
\frac{V}{X_{m}}\] \[I_{\mu}
=
\frac{400}{1200.088}\] \[I_{\mu}=0.334\,\text{A}\] Core loss current: \[I_{w}
=
\frac{P_{c}}{V}\] \[I_{w}
=
\frac{80}{400}\] \[I_{w}=0.2\,\text{A}\] Since both currents are orthogonal, \[I_{0}
=
\sqrt{I_{\mu}^{2}+I_{w}^{2}}\] \[I_{0}
=
\sqrt{(0.334)^2+(0.2)^2}\] \[I_{0}=0.389\,\text{A}\] \[\boxed{0.389}\]

Given: \[V_{t}=220\,\text{V}, \qquad R_{a}=2.5\,\Omega\] Armature voltage, field current and losses are assumed to remain constant from no-load to full-load. Rated armature current: \[(I_{a})_{\text{rated}}=8\,\text{A}\] Full-load copper loss: \[P_{Cu}=I_{a,\text{rated}}^{2}R_{a}
=8^{2}\times 2.5
=160\,\text{W}\] Under full-load condition, \[E_{1}=220-8\times 2.5=200\,\text{V}\] Developed power: \[P_{\text{dev}}=E_{1}I_{a}
=200\times 8
=1600\,\text{W}\] Rated shaft power: \[P_{sh}=1500\,\text{W}\] Therefore, constant losses: \[P_{\text{const}}=1600-1500=100\,\text{W}\] At no-load, developed power equals constant losses: \[E_{b}I_{a}
=(V_{t}-I_{a}R_{a})I_{a}
=100\] \[(220-2.5I_{a})I_{a}=100\] \[220I_{a}-2.5I_{a}^{2}=100\] \[2.5I_{a}^{2}-220I_{a}+100=0\] Solving, \[I_{a}=0.4569\,\text{A}\] Hence, induced EMF at no-load: \[E_{2}=220-0.4569\times 2.5
=218.86\,\text{V}\] Since field current is constant, flux remains constant and \[E\propto N\] Therefore, \[\frac{E_{2}}{E_{1}}=\frac{N_{2}}{N_{1}}\] \[N_{2}
=\frac{218.86}{200}\times 859
=939.99\,\text{rpm}\] \[\boxed{940}\]

When one transformer of a delta-delta transformer bank is removed, the remaining two transformers operate in open-delta or V-V connection. In open-delta connection, three-phase supply is still available at the motor terminals. Hence, single phasing will not occur. The capacity of an open-delta bank is \[\text{Open-delta capacity}
=
57.7\% \text{ of closed-delta capacity}\] Therefore, the induction motor can be loaded only up to \[57.7\%\] of its rating, with balanced three-phase voltage at its terminals. Hence, the correct option is \[\boxed{C}\]

Given, \[f_1 = 50\,\text{Hz}, \qquad P = 4\] The original synchronous speed is \[N_{s1}=\frac{120f_1}{P}
=\frac{120\times 50}{4}
=1500\,\text{rpm}\] The motor speed is \[N_1=1490\,\text{rpm}\] Hence, the original slip is \[s_1=\frac{N_{s1}-N_1}{N_{s1}}
=\frac{1500-1490}{1500}
=0.00667
=0.67\%\] The motor is required to run at \[N_2=1600\,\text{rpm}\] For small-slip operation, \[T \propto sV^2\] Since the load torque and applied voltage remain unchanged, the slip remains approximately constant. Therefore, \[s_2=s_1=0.00667\] Using \[N_2=N_{s2}(1-s_2)\] \[N_{s2}=\frac{N_2}{1-s_2}
=\frac{1600}{1-0.00667}
=1610.7\,\text{rpm}\] Now, \[f_2=\frac{N_{s2}P}{120}
=\frac{1610.7\times 4}{120}
=53.72\,\text{Hz}\] Hence, \[\boxed{53.72}\]

Given: \[\text{Number of stator slots} = 36\] \[\text{Phase spread} = m\beta = 120^\circ\] Slots per pole per phase, \[m=\frac{36}{8\times 3}=1.5\] Since \(m\) is not an integer, it is a fractional slot winding. \[m=\frac{\text{slots/ph}}{\text{pole}}
=\frac{36/3}{8}
=\frac{4\times 3}{4\times 2}
=\frac{Ks}{Kp}\] Here, \(K\) is the HCF and \[s=3\] For a fractional slot winding, the distribution factor is \[k_d=
\frac{\sin\left(\dfrac{m\beta}{2}\right)}
{s\sin\left(\dfrac{m\beta}{2s}\right)}\] Substituting, \[k_d=
\frac{\sin\left(\dfrac{120^\circ}{2}\right)}
{3\sin\left(\dfrac{120^\circ}{2\times 3}\right)}\] \[k_d=
\frac{\sin 60^\circ}
{3\sin 20^\circ}\] \[k_d=
\frac{0.8660}{3\times 0.3420}\] \[k_d=0.844\] \[\boxed{0.844}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For fractional slot windings, slots per pole per phase (\(m\)) is non-integer.</li><li>Distribution factor for fractional slot winding is \[k_d=\frac{\sin\left(\dfrac{m\beta}{2}\right)}
{s\sin\left(\dfrac{m\beta}{2s}\right)}\] where \(s\) is obtained from the fractional-slot grouping.</li><li>Distribution factor is always less than unity.</li></ul></div>

The cost of electrical energy generation depends strongly on how effectively the installed generating capacity is utilized. The load factor is defined as \[\text{Load Factor}
=
\frac{\text{Average Load}}
{\text{Maximum Demand}}\] A high load factor indicates that the generating plant is utilized more uniformly throughout the day. This improves utilization of equipment and reduces the cost per unit of generated energy. The diversity factor is defined as \[\text{Diversity Factor}
=
\frac{\sum \text{Individual Maximum Demands}}
{\text{Simultaneous Maximum Demand}}\] A higher diversity factor means that all consumers do not reach their maximum demand at the same instant. Consequently, the installed generation capacity required is reduced. Therefore, <ul><li>High load factor improves plant utilization.</li><li>High diversity factor reduces installed capacity requirements.</li><li>Both effects reduce the overall cost of generation.</li></ul> Hence, for minimum generation cost, \[\boxed{\text{(D)}\;
\text{Load factor and diversity factor should be high}}\]

A double line-to-ground (LLG) fault is an unsymmetrical fault involving two phases and ground. For such faults, all three sequence networks participate: <ul><li>Positive-sequence network</li><li>Negative-sequence network</li><li>Zero-sequence network</li></ul> Since the alternator neutral is solidly grounded, the zero-sequence current is allowed to flow and therefore the zero-sequence network must be included in the fault analysis. The generator is unloaded and operating at rated voltage before the fault. Hence the prefault positive-sequence voltage is \[E_{a1}=1\angle0^\circ\ \text{p.u.}\] The given sequence reactances are \[X_1=0.25\ \text{p.u.}\] \[X_2=0.35\ \text{p.u.}\] \[X_0=0.10\ \text{p.u.}\] For a double line-to-ground fault, the positive-sequence current is \[I_{a1}
=
\frac{E_{a1}}
{Z_1+\left(Z_2 \parallel Z_0\right)}\] The parallel combination of the negative- and zero-sequence impedances is \[Z_2\parallel Z_0
=
\frac{Z_2Z_0}
{Z_2+Z_0}\] Substituting the given values, \[Z_2\parallel Z_0
=
\frac{0.35\times0.10}
{0.35+0.10}\] \[=
\frac{0.035}{0.45}\] \[=
0.0778\] Therefore, \[I_{a1}
=
\frac{1}
{0.25+0.0778}\] \[=
\frac{1}{0.3278}\] \[=
3.05\ \text{p.u.}\] For a double line-to-ground fault, the fault current is related to the zero-sequence current by \[I_f=3I_0\] The zero-sequence current can be expressed in terms of the positive-sequence current as \[I_0
=
I_{a1}
\left(
\frac{Z_2}{Z_2+Z_0}
\right)\] Substituting the values, \[I_f
=
3\times3.05
\left(
\frac{0.35}{0.35+0.10}
\right)\] \[=
3\times3.05
\left(
\frac{0.35}{0.45}
\right)\] \[=
7.12\ \text{p.u.}\] Thus the fault current in per-unit is \[\boxed{I_f=7.12\ \text{p.u.}}\] --- To convert the current into actual amperes, first determine the base current. The generator rating is \[S_{base}=30\ \text{MVA}\] and \[V_{base}=13.8\ \text{kV}\] Hence \[I_{base}
=
\frac{S_{base}}
{\sqrt3\,V_{base}}\] \[=
\frac{30\times10^6}
{\sqrt3\times13.8\times10^3}\] \[=
1255.2\ \text{A}\] The positive-sequence current in actual units is \[I_{a1(actual)}
=
3.05\times1255.2\] \[=
3828\ \text{A}\] \[=
3.828\ \text{kA}\] The fault current in actual units becomes \[I_f
=
7.12\times1255.2\] \[=
8932.8\ \text{A}\] \[=
8.93\ \text{kA}\] Therefore, the required answer is \[\boxed{I_f=7.12\ \text{p.u.}}\]\[\boxed{I_f=8.93\ \text{kA}}\]

Voltage drop is the primary design criterion for sizing a distributor in a radial distribution system. Unlike transmission lines, a distributor has loads tapped at various points along its length. Consequently, the current varies from one section of the distributor to another, causing different voltage drops along the feeder. To ensure that all consumers receive voltage within the prescribed statutory limits, the conductor cross- sectional area is selected such that the voltage drop from the feeding point to the farthest consumer remains within the allowable limit. Although factors such as capital cost, temperature rise, and corona loss are important in power system design, they are not the governing criterion for selecting the size of a radial distributor. Therefore, the correct answer is \[\boxed{
\text{(A)}\;
\text{Voltage drop}
}\]

The natural load of a transmission line or cable is the load at which reactive power generated by capacitance equals reactive power absorbed by inductance. Underground cables possess: <ul><li>Very high capacitance</li><li>Relatively low inductance</li></ul> Consequently, their natural load is extremely high. However, the thermal rating of the cable limits the actual load that can be carried continuously. The conductor temperature, insulation temperature, and heat dissipation capability determine the rated load. Therefore, \[\text{Rated Load}
&lt;
\text{Natural Load}\] Hence, \[\boxed{\text{LESS}}\]

The transient recovery voltage appearing across a circuit breaker immediately after current interruption can be represented by an equivalent RLC circuit. For a shunt resistor connected across the breaker contacts, the condition for critical damping is \[R_c
=
\frac{1}{2}
\sqrt{\frac{L}{C}}.\] Given, \[L=1~\text{H}\] and \[C=0.01~\mu\text{F}
=
10^{-8}~\text{F}.\] Substituting, \[R_c
=
\frac{1}{2}
\sqrt{\frac{1}{10^{-8}}}.\] Since \[\sqrt{10^{8}}
=
10^{4},\] we obtain \[R_c
=
\frac{10^{4}}{2}
=
5000~\Omega.\] Therefore, the value of the shunt resistor required for critical damping of the re-striking voltage is \[\boxed{5000~\Omega}\]

For a three-phase diode bridge rectifier, \[(V_o)_{\text{avg}}
=
\frac{3V_m}{\pi}\] where \[V_m=\sqrt{2}\,V_{LL}\] Given, \[V_{LL}=440\,\text{V}\] Hence, \[V_m
=
440\sqrt{2}
=
622.25\,\text{V}\] Therefore, \[(V_o)_{\text{avg}}
=
